<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec_polarization" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  	<title>Polarization</title>

	<assemblage component="implementation">
  <title>Implementation Notes</title>
  <p>
	The activities in this section and the previous section (<xref ref="sec_em-waves-reps"/>) were designed to be implemented in an interactive studio classroom, after students have completed the previous sections in the chapter.  They take about two hours.
	</p>
  </assemblage>

  <subsubsection>
	<title>Activities</title>

<activity>
	<title>Polarizers</title>
	<introduction>
		<p>
	
	</p>
	<p>
		A polarizer is a device that selectively blocks electromagnetic waves: only waves with an electric field in a single direction are able to pass through.
	</p>

	</introduction>
	
		<task>
			<p>
				Use a polarizer to inspect different sources of light in the room.
			</p>
			<p>
				<ol>
					<li>
						<p>
							How does what you see through the polarizer compare to what you can see without it?
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Are there any sources of light that behave differently?  Check a variety of sources, including overhead lights and lights from different electronic screens
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</p>
		</task>
		<task>
			<p>
				Use two polarizers to inspect sources of light in the room.
			</p>
			<p>
				<ol>
					<li>
						<p>
							Make a list of your observations.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Arrange the polarizers so that no light is visible through them, and use your model for electromagnetic light to explain this observation.
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</p>
		</task>
		<task>
			<p>
				Use three polarizers to inspect sources of light in the room.  See if you can find a way to add a third polarizer to the two-polarizer setup in the previous question that will allow you to see something through the polarizers.  What do you think is happening?
			</p>
		</task>
</activity>



</subsubsection>

	


	
	
<!--NO AI-->
</section>

